    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Origin unclear. Used in ancient texts to mean \"white\" and \"hundred\" (百)."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "西方色也。陰用事，物色白。从入合二。二，陰數。凡白之屬皆从白。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Unclear; probably a pictogram (象形).\nGuo (1954) suggests that it represents a thumb and is original form of 拇 (*mɯʔ) or, more probably, 擘 (*preːɡ).\nAlternatively, Unger (apud Schuessler, 2007) suggests that it represents an acorn, noting its use in 樂 /乐 (“oak”) (= 櫟 /栎) and 皂 (zào, “acorn”). In this interpretation, the meaning of “white” is derived from the color of an acorn's inside. Unrelated to the top component of 原.\nSTEDT derives it from Proto-Sino-Tibetan *bawk (“white”), whence also Proto-Bodo-Garo *bok (“white”), Limbu ᤑ (phô, “white”); also compare Proto-Mon-Khmer *kɓɔɔk (“white, grey”).\nHowever, Schuessler (2007) finds it difficult to reconcile these forms with Middle Chinese due to the lack of medial r. Instead, he proposes a derivation from Proto-Sino-Tibetan *bar (“white”) with -k extension, thus cognate with 皤 (OC *baːl, “white”) (Wang, 1982), Mizo vâr (“(to be) white”), Proto-Karen *ʔ(b)wa (“white”)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "西方色也。陰用事，物色白。从入合二。二，陰數。凡白之屬皆从白。",
